theory.intro:
The **domain** is every allowed input $x$; the **range** is every output $y$. On a graph the domain is the shadow on the $x$-axis, the range the shadow on the $y$-axis. Write each as an **inequality** and in **interval notation**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0)--(5.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.1)--(0,4.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[gray,dashed] (1,1)--(1,0);
\draw[gray,dashed] (1,1)--(0,1);
\draw[gray,dashed] (4,3.5)--(4,0);
\draw[gray,dashed] (4,3.5)--(0,3.5);
\draw[thick,domain=1:4,smooth,samples=60,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1+0.2778*(\x-1)*(\x-1)});
\fill[black] (1,1) circle (2.5pt);
\fill[black] (4,3.5) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[blue,line width=2pt] (1,0)--(4,0);
\draw[red,line width=2pt] (0,1)--(0,3.5);
\node[below] at (1,-0.12) {$a$};
\node[below] at (4,-0.12) {$b$};
\node[left] at (-0.14,1) {$c$};
\node[left] at (-0.14,3.5) {$d$};
\node[blue,below] at (2.5,-0.85) {domain};
\node[red,rotate=90] at (-1.1,2.25) {range};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
An open endpoint $\circ$ is **excluded**, shown by $<$ or $>$ and a round bracket.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0)--(5.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.1)--(0,4.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[gray,dashed] (1,1)--(1,0);
\draw[gray,dashed] (4,3.5)--(4,0);
\draw[thick,domain=1:4,smooth,samples=60,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1+0.2778*(\x-1)*(\x-1)});
\fill[black] (1,1) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[thick,fill=white] (4,3.5) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[blue,line width=2pt] (1,0)--(4,0);
\fill[blue] (1,0) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[blue,line width=1pt,fill=white] (4,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below] at (1,-0.16) {$a$};
\node[below] at (4,-0.16) {$b$};
\node at (1.9,3.2) {$a \leq x < b$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An arrow means the graph continues, not that a finite endpoint is excluded. Check how the continuing graph behaves on each axis: it may extend without bound or approach a finite boundary without reaching it. For example, an exponential graph can continue left forever while approaching $y=0$. Use a round bracket at an excluded finite boundary and always at $-\infty$ or $\infty$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0)--(5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.1)--(0,4.7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[gray,dashed] (1,1)--(1,0);
\draw[thick,->,domain=1:4.3,smooth,samples=60,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1+0.2778*(\x-1)*(\x-1)});
\fill[black] (1,1) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[blue,line width=2pt,->] (1,0)--(4.9,0);
\fill[blue] (1,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below] at (1,-0.16) {$a$};
\node at (1.9,3.2) {$x \geq a$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Write the domain and range of the graph in inequality and interval notation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2)--(0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick] (-2,-1)--(3,3);
\fill[black] (-2,-1) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (3,3) circle (2pt);
\path (-2,-1) node[below left] {$(-2,-1)$} (3,3) node[above left] {$(3,3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The shadow on the $x$-axis extends from $-2$ to $3$. Both endpoints are included.

Domain: $-2\leq x\leq3$, or $[-2,3]$.

The shadow on the $y$-axis extends from $-1$ to $3$, including both boundaries.

Range: $-1\leq y\leq3$, or $[-1,3]$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{shadow}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2)--(0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick] (-2,-1)--(3,3);
\fill[black] (-2,-1) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (3,3) circle (2pt);
\path (-2,-1) node[below left] {$(-2,-1)$} (3,3) node[above left] {$(3,3)$};
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick] (-2,0)--(3,0);
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick] (0,-1)--(0,3);
\foreach \p in {(-2,0),(3,0),(0,-1),(0,3)} \fill[shadow] \p circle (2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

