workedExamples.question_text:
Write the domain and range of the line in interval notation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-2.5,2.5)--(2.5,-2.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The line continues without bound to the left and right, using every real input. Its outputs also extend without bound in both directions.

Domain: $x\in\mathbb{R}$, or $(-\infty,\infty)$.

Range: $y\in\mathbb{R}$, or $(-\infty,\infty)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{shadow}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-2.5,2.5)--(2.5,-2.5);
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick,<->] (-2.7,0)--(2.7,0);
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick,<->] (0,-2.7)--(0,2.7);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Write the domain and range of the parabola in inequality and interval notation where applicable.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->,domain=-2.2:2.2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-(\x)*(\x)});
\path (0,2) node[above right] {$(0,2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Both branches continue, so every real $x$ is allowed.

Domain: $x\in\mathbb{R}$, or $(-\infty,\infty)$.

The turning point $(0,2)$ gives the highest output. It is included, and the outputs extend downward without bound.

Range: $y\leq2$, or $(-\infty,2]$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{shadow}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->,domain=-2.2:2.2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-(\x)*(\x)});
\path (0,2) node[above right] {$(0,2)$};
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick,<->] (-2.7,0)--(2.7,0);
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick,<-] (0,-2.7)--(0,2);
\fill[shadow] (0,2) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The exponential graph has the $x$-axis as an asymptote. Write its domain and range in interval notation, and its range as an inequality.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-1)--(0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->,domain=-2.5:2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(\x*ln(2))});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The graph continues for every real input.

Domain: $x\in\mathbb{R}$, or $(-\infty,\infty)$.

The outputs remain positive. They approach $0$ without reaching it and increase without bound.

Range: $y>0$, or $(0,\infty)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{shadow}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-1)--(0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->,domain=-2.5:2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(\x*ln(2))});
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick,<->] (-2.7,0)--(2.7,0);
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick,->] (0,0)--(0,4.7);
\draw[shadow,fill=white,thick] (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) At the excluded boundary $x=4$, the output would be $4^2=16$. For $x>4$, the curve rises from this open boundary without bound.

Range: $y>16$, or $(16,\infty)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=2mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{shadow}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-0.5,0)--(6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2)--(0,32) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,->,domain=4:5.5,samples=60,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x)*(\x)});
\draw[black,fill=white,thick] (4,16) circle (2.5pt);
\path (4,16) node[right] {$(4,16)$};
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick,->] (0,16)--(0,30);
\draw[shadow,fill=white,thick] (0,16) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(b) The turning point $(0,0)$ lies inside the domain and gives the minimum output $0$. Both endpoints give the maximum output $2^2=4$. Both boundaries are included.

Range: $0\leq y\leq4$, or $[0,4]$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{shadow}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-1)--(0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,domain=-2:2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x)*(\x)});
\fill[black] (-2,4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill[black] (2,4) circle (2.5pt);
\path (-2,4) node[above] {$(-2,4)$} (2,4) node[above] {$(2,4)$} (0,0) node[below left] {$(0,0)$};
\draw[shadow,dashed,thick] (0,0)--(0,4);
\fill[shadow] (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill[shadow] (0,4) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

